WEBVTT

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:02.979
Core OT security begins with asset inventory.

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:06.875
Record devices, software, firmware, communication paths, owners, vendors,

00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:11.165
physical locations, operational functions, and critical dependencies.

00:00:11.165 --> 00:00:13.005
Inventory is not merely a spreadsheet exercise.

00:00:13.005 --> 00:00:17.385
It supports segmentation, monitoring, vulnerability decisions, incident response,

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:19.735
backup planning, and lifecycle management.

00:00:19.735 --> 00:00:22.360
Secure remote access through managed entry points.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.570
Use individual identities, strong authentication, least privilege, approval, time limits,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.335
and session monitoring where feasible.

00:00:28.335 --> 00:00:30.705
Remove dormant pathways and shared credentials.

00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:34.796
Coordinate emergency access procedures in advance so operational urgency does not force

00:00:34.796 --> 00:00:36.035
insecure improvisation.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:40.555
Network monitoring provides visibility without necessarily touching sensitive devices.

00:00:40.555 --> 00:00:44.700
Establish a baseline of expected communications, then investigate new connections,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.400
unusual protocols, unauthorized devices, configuration transfers,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.400
and traffic crossing trust boundaries.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.635
Monitoring must be interpreted with operational context;

00:00:53.635 --> 00:00:56.310
maintenance activity may look unusual but be fully authorized.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.530
Segmentation limits pathways, while access control limits who and what can use them.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.190
Apply role-based privileges to operator, engineering, administrative,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.210
and vendor functions.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.100
Protect privileged workstations and management interfaces.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.240
Review accounts when personnel, contracts, and job responsibilities change.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.165
Backups should cover controller logic, device configurations, HMI and SCADA applications,

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:20.395
historian settings, servers, and the documentation needed to rebuild.

00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:23.680
Store protected copies, verify integrity, and practice restoration.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.780
Recovery plans must account for physical process state and the order in which systems can

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.090
safely return to service.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.865
Vulnerability management in OT combines vendor advisories, asset context, exposure,

00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:37.355
compensating controls, test results, and maintenance planning.

00:01:37.355 --> 00:01:40.860
Change control ensures that patches, configuration updates, firewall rules,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.040
and logic changes are reviewed, tested, authorized, documented, and reversible.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.630
Finally, incident response plans should define roles for security personnel, operators,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.020
engineers, safety teams, leadership, communications, and vendors.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.130
Practice scenarios involving loss of visibility, remote-access misuse, ransomware,

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.860
and control-system disruption.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.385
The goal is coordinated decision-making that protects people and operations while

00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:04.600
preserving evidence and restoring trusted control.
